WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
A desert locust living alone is green and timid, and it shies away from others of its kind.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:10.340
But touch its hind legs for a few hours, and its color begins to change.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:15.430
It starts seeking out the crowd, and it becomes part of a swarm billions strong.

4
00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:20.040
What triggers this transformation is neither a gene nor a virus.

5
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.290
The trigger is a single chemical, and that same chemical is found in our own brains.

6
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:39.700
For centuries, a farmer standing at the edge of his field believed he was looking at two different insects.

7
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.630
One was greenish or sand-colored, shy, and solitary.

8
00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.560
It hid among the grasses and moved away whenever it spotted another of its kind.

9
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.730
Farmers usually considered it harmless.

10
00:00:52.730 --> 00:01:00.700
The other seemed like a different creature altogether: dark, spotted with black, tinged with yellow or orange, always on the move.

11
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.470
Thousands, even millions of them would gather until they darkened the sky.

12
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.680
It was a catastrophe.

13
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.010
Entomologists thought the same way the farmers did.

14
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:17.860
These were two separate species, with separate names, and they sat in museum drawers under separate labels.

15
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.950
The man who overturned this picture was a Russian entomologist named Boris Uvarov.

16
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:28.680
Uvarov was born in eighteen eighty-eight in the town of Uralsk, in what is now Kazakhstan.

17
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.770
As a young man he worked on agricultural pests in the Caucasus and southern Russia.

18
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:38.060
He knew locust outbreaks firsthand, from the middle of the field.

19
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:47.310
After the revolution and the civil war, he settled in London in nineteen twenty and began working on the locust collections at the Imperial Bureau of Entomology there.

20
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:57.200
Two species lay before him: the migratory locust, Locusta migratoria, and a calmer, more scattered species of the same genus, Locusta danica.

21
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.450
As Uvarov examined the specimens one by one, he noticed something unsettling.

22
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.420
There was no sharp boundary between the two species.

23
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:15.510
At one end stood the typical migratory form, at the other the typical calm form, and an unbroken series of intermediate forms filled the space between them.

24
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:25.080
The shape of the ridge on the back of the thorax, the length of the wings relative to the legs, the color patterns: all of these shifted step by step from one form to the other.

25
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.690
Field observations by his colleagues in Russia pointed in the same direction.

26
00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:37.340
In outbreak years the migratory form multiplied, and when the outbreak died down, the calm form remained in the region.

27
00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.350
It was as if one were turning into the other.

28
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.600
In a paper published in nineteen twenty-one, Uvarov reached a bold conclusion.

29
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.370
These were not two species but two different states of the same species.

30
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:59.460
He called these states "phases." He named the solitary form the solitarious phase and the swarming form the gregarious phase.

31
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.910
In his view, what decided the matter was not genes but conditions.

32
00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:07.560
When locusts were sparse, they stayed solitarious.

33
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.370
When they became crowded, they turned gregarious.

34
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:18.060
This idea also explained why locust plagues erupted in certain years and then vanished for years at a time.

35
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.550
Disaster did not fall from the sky.

36
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:25.360
It built up slowly within a quiet population.

37
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.530
The idea was soon put to the test.

38
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:37.620
In the early nineteen-twenties, the entomologist Faure, working in South Africa, reared the young of the local brown locust at different densities.

39
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:45.430
Those raised in crowded containers took on the colors and behavior of the swarming form, while those raised one by one stayed solitarious.

40
00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.560
Now Uvarov's theory could be seen in the laboratory as well.

41
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.690
Uvarov devoted the rest of his life to locusts.

42
00:03:53.690 --> 00:04:02.300
The Anti-Locust Research Centre in London, which he was instrumental in founding, remained for many years the hub of the world's knowledge about locusts.

43
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:08.990
Today this discovery stands as one of the most striking examples of what we call phenotypic plasticity.

44
00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:16.320
The same genetic code can, depending on the environment, give rise to two bodies and two temperaments that look nothing alike.

45
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:24.290
The migratory locust has this duality, and so does the desert locust, which lives across a broad belt stretching from Africa to India.

46
00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:29.700
The desert locust became the species scientists studied most in the decades that followed.

47
00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:37.190
But one question remained open, a question Uvarov could not answer, and perhaps could never have answered with the tools of his time.

48
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.840
What exactly did crowding do to a locust?

49
00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:48.010
How does an animal "know" that it is surrounded by many of its own kind, and what switch does that knowledge flip inside the body?

50
00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:53.740
The answer emerged step by step nearly eighty years later, in laboratories in England.

51
00:04:53.740 --> 00:05:02.350
Researchers including Stephen Simpson at Oxford and Steve Rogers and Malcolm Burrows at Cambridge began by measuring how fast the transformation happens.

52
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:05.040
The result was surprising.

53
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:13.970
Physical changes, such as the shift in color and in body proportions, took weeks, and some were fully established only over several generations.

54
00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:17.140
Behavior, however, changed far more quickly.

55
00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:24.310
Put a solitarious desert locust into a crowded cage, and within just a few hours it became a different animal.

56
00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:33.080
The insect that had first fled from its own kind soon began to approach them, to move about more, and to behave at ease in the middle of the crowd.

57
00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.730
So what was the locust actually sensing in a crowd?

58
00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:40.380
The researchers separated the senses one at a time.

59
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:49.310
Showing a locust the sight and smell of other locusts together had some effect, but the strongest trigger turned out to come from somewhere else entirely: touch.

60
00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:58.080
In a study published in two thousand and one, Simpson and his team used a small paintbrush to stroke different parts of a locust's body at regular intervals.

61
00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.210
Touching its back, its belly or its antennae changed very little.

62
00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:09.700
But touching the thick upper part of the hind leg, the outer face of the thigh, pushed a solitarious locust toward swarming behavior.

63
00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:14.470
In the wild, this is the unavoidable consequence of living in a crowd.

64
00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:22.120
Locusts gathered around scarce patches of vegetation are constantly brushing against one another, their legs knocking together.

65
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:29.610
Every neighbor that touches a leg sends the same message to the nervous system: you are not alone, there are many others around you.

66
00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:34.700
The next step was to find out what this message changes in the brain and the nerve centers.

67
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.150
An insect's nervous system is organized differently from ours.

68
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:44.720
There is a brain in the head, and along the body there are clusters of nerve cells called ganglia.

69
00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.330
The ganglia that control the movement of the legs sit in the thorax.

70
00:06:49.330 --> 00:07:00.020
A team made up of Michael Anstey, Steve Rogers, Swidbert Ott, Malcolm Burrows and Stephen Simpson measured the chemicals in these thoracic ganglia in locusts exposed to crowding.

71
00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:06.070
The result, published in two thousand and nine, quickly caught the attention of the scientific world.

72
00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:13.560
In the first hours after swarming behavior began, the amount of serotonin in the thoracic ganglia rose roughly threefold.

73
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.530
Serotonin is an ancient signaling molecule.

74
00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:25.020
In the human brain it is linked to mood, sleep and appetite, and in many animals it performs a range of different jobs.

75
00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.990
In the locust, it turned out to play the key role.

76
00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:36.000
To show that the connection was no coincidence, the researchers tested it from both directions.

77
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:44.610
Locusts given substances that blocked the effect of serotonin kept their solitarious behavior, even when they were exposed to crowds or to touches on the leg.

78
00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:56.260
Conversely, when solitarious locusts that had never seen a crowd were given serotonin, or substances that boosted its production, these animals began to behave like gregarious ones, even though they were alone.

79
00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:00.390
In other words, serotonin was not a by-product.

80
00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.880
It was the switch that started the transformation.

81
00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:06.570
There is an important detail here.

82
00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.580
The rise in serotonin was temporary.

83
00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:16.430
It set the transformation in motion, but it did not stay high once swarming behavior had gone on for a long time.

84
00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:23.600
Other processes took over to make the gregarious phase permanent, and these processes reshaped the nervous system itself.

85
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:32.850
According to measurements by Ott and Rogers in twenty ten, the brains of gregarious desert locusts were about thirty percent larger than those of solitarious ones,

86
00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:38.260
and the proportions of the brain regions relative to one another were different as well.

87
00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:41.910
Researchers are still interpreting what this difference means.

88
00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:51.800
One idea is that an animal living in a crowded and changeable environment, constantly on the move and dealing with a wider range of food, may need to process more information.

89
00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:56.410
But this is a well-supported explanation rather than a settled conclusion.

90
00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.540
The change is also passed from one generation to the next.

91
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:07.550
When a female locust living in crowded conditions lays her eggs in the soil, she covers them with a foamy secretion.

92
00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:17.440
Research has shown that the density the mother experienced is passed on to her offspring this way, and that the young of mothers who lived in crowds hatch with gregarious traits already in place.

93
00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.730
The chemical details of this transfer are still being investigated.

94
00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:30.820
The upshot is this: if a locust population stays crowded for several generations, each new generation is born more ready for the transformation.

95
00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:38.150
The young are marked with black and bright yellow or orange patterns, while the adults turn pink at first and then yellow once they mature.

96
00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:41.000
These bright colors may serve a purpose too.

97
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:49.450
Gregarious locusts do not shy away from eating toxic plants, and the substances they take in from those plants can make them unpalatable to some predators.

98
00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:56.460
Unlike their solitarious cousins, which try to stay hidden, these eye-catching colors may work as a warning: don't eat me.

99
00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:05.070
In short, a chain that begins with ordinary touches on a leg goes on to change behavior, the brain, color, diet and the next generation.

100
00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:09.040
Yet individually transformed locusts are not yet a swarm.

101
00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:15.890
The truly frightening moment comes when these individuals come together and begin to move as a single living thing.

102
00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:20.980
Young locusts whose wings have not yet developed cannot fly, but they can walk.

103
00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:26.870
Nymphs that have entered the gregarious phase gather together to form marching crowds known as bands.

104
00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:34.360
These bands sometimes stretch for kilometers, advancing in the same direction for days and sweeping away the vegetation in their path.

105
00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.490
For a long time, no one knew how this order came about.

106
00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:43.260
There was no leader, and no one was pointing the way.

107
00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:47.870
And yet thousands of insects turned in the same direction and marched on.

108
00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:56.640
In two thousand six, a team that included Jerome Buhl, Stephen Simpson and the physicist Iain Couzin took this question into the laboratory.

109
00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:02.050
They placed young locust nymphs in a ring-shaped arena and gradually increased their numbers.

110
00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:06.500
What they saw looked a lot like what physicists call a phase transition.

111
00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:11.110
When there were only a few locusts, each one wandered at random.

112
00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:18.280
As the density rose, brief moments of alignment began to appear, and groups would switch direction every so often.

113
00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:26.570
But above a certain threshold, which in this experiment came at around seventy-four locusts per square metre, the disorder suddenly vanished.

114
00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:32.940
Almost the entire crowd began marching in the same direction, for long stretches, without turning back.

115
00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:40.430
Just as water turns abruptly to ice at a certain temperature, the crowd's behaviour changed in a sudden leap rather than step by step.

116
00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.760
So what was driving them to march?

117
00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:48.210
The answer showed that a locust swarm is no romantic act of solidarity.

118
00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:55.380
A two thousand eight study by Sepideh Bazazi and her colleagues revealed that the marching bands are driven by the fear of cannibalism.

119
00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.670
Migrating nymphs are especially hungry for protein and salt.

120
00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:04.280
And the nearest source of protein is the fellow locust marching just ahead.

121
00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:11.770
Locusts in the crowd try to bite the abdomen of the one in front, while the one in front moves forward to escape the attack from behind.

122
00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:22.780
The researchers found that when they reduced sensation in the abdomen, that is, the ability to sense a fellow locust approaching from behind, the insects became less inclined to move and were bitten more often.

123
00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:28.670
In locusts fed on protein and salt, on the other hand, the cannibalistic tendency declined.

124
00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:33.920
A marching band is, so to speak, an army fleeing from whatever is behind it.

125
00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.050
Each individual chases the one ahead while running from the one behind.

126
00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:41.700
And this forced march pushes the whole crowd forward.

127
00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.710
That is why the swarm never stops.

128
00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:48.840
Marching is both a way to find food and a way to avoid being eaten.

129
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:57.770
As the crowd grows, food runs short; as food runs short, hunger and cannibalism increase, and that in turn speeds up the march.

130
00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:03.020
When the nymphs reach adulthood and grow wings, the same drive is carried into the air.

131
00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:10.510
Solitary desert locusts usually fly alone and at night, but gregarious adults take off by day in vast swarms.

132
00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:17.200
Carried by the wind, they can cover more than a hundred kilometres a day, and sometimes more than a hundred and fifty.

133
00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:21.650
From this point on, the numbers begin to strain human comprehension.

134
00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:30.580
According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, a single square kilometre of swarm can hold between forty and eighty million locusts.

135
00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:36.470
Every adult locust eats roughly its own weight in fresh vegetation each day, about two grams.

136
00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:46.520
By a comparison the Food and Agriculture Organization often uses, a swarm covering one square kilometre eats as much food in a single day as about thirty-five thousand people.

137
00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:51.450
Real swarms, however, often cover hundreds of square kilometres.

138
00:13:51.450 --> 00:14:01.340
In major plague years, the desert locust can affect about twenty percent of the world's land surface, with the affected region stretching across more than sixty countries.

139
00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:05.150
The endurance of these swarms is astonishing, too.

140
00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:13.600
In nineteen eighty-eight, desert locust swarms that took off from West Africa crossed the Atlantic Ocean and reached the Caribbean in about ten days.

141
00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.250
That was a journey of roughly five thousand kilometres.

142
00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:23.460
A more recent example is the upsurge that struck East Africa between twenty nineteen and twenty twenty-one.

143
00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:32.390
Unusual rainfall in Yemen and across the Arabian Peninsula first caused quiet populations to explode, and then set off the transformation.

144
00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:39.720
Kenya suffered its worst locust invasion in decades, and the swarms spread from Ethiopia and Somalia all the way to South Asia.

145
00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:50.090
The process Uvarov had described a hundred years earlier was working in exactly the same way, even in the age of satellite imagery and crop-spraying aircraft.

146
00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:57.740
It is precisely here, in the logic of locust control, that the most lasting legacy of Uvarov's phase theory lies.

147
00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:03.150
Once a swarm has taken wing, catching up with it is very difficult and very expensive.

148
00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:10.000
But every swarm was once made up of solitary locusts, avoiding one another in some remote corner of the desert.

149
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:20.530
That is why today the Food and Agriculture Organization keeps constant watch over rainfall, vegetation and locust density in the areas where locusts live, even when there is no plague.

150
00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:27.540
National field teams try to spot early the places where plants are turning green and locusts are starting to crowd together.

151
00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:33.430
The goal is to stop a population on the verge of transformation before bands and swarms can form.

152
00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:43.800
Alongside chemical pesticides, biological control agents made from certain species of fungi, which target only locusts, have also become part of this strategy.

153
00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:48.730
When early warning works, a disaster is averted without the farmer ever knowing.

154
00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:53.340
When it fails, swarms cross borders, as they did in twenty nineteen.

155
00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:58.270
For science, though, the locust became a window onto a much broader question.

156
00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:07.680
That two entirely different personalities could emerge from the very same genes, that a brief surge in a single signaling molecule could transform an entire way of life,

157
00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:17.890
that thousands of independent individuals could flow in one direction without any leader: all of this drew not only biologists but also physicists and mathematicians to this insect.

158
00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:28.420
The models built to explain locust bands speak the same language as research that tries to understand many different forms of collective behavior, from schools of fish to flocks of birds,

159
00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:31.750
and even the movement of large human crowds.

160
00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.800
Here, we need to be careful.

161
00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:40.330
The fact that serotonin is also found in humans does not mean that human crowds behave like locusts.

162
00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:43.660
No such conclusion is supported by science.

163
00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:51.630
What they share are the mathematical tools that help us understand how large-scale order emerges from the simple rules followed by individuals.

164
00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:57.680
Perhaps the most striking part of this story is how small the thing is that sets the disaster in motion.

165
00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:02.130
A few clumps of plants turning green in a desert after the rain.

166
00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:08.660
A few hundred locusts crowded around those plants, brushing against one another's hind legs.

167
00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:13.910
A surge of serotonin lasting a few hours in the nerve clusters of their thorax.

168
00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:23.000
Then animals that begin to draw closer together, bands that swell in number, young hoppers chasing one another, and finally a cloud that darkens the sky.

169
00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:30.970
This is what Uvarov saw when he looked at an insect we had taken for two separate species: the monster was not coming from somewhere else.

170
00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:36.380
It lay waiting inside the calm insect, and a single touch was enough to wake it.

171
00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:40.190
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

172
00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:42.880
Until we meet again in a new story.
